From eae3f6f9e0362f4dcacf4bcdd6d8f74601f5ff9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:51:51 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 33/41] Frame interpolation: draws that sample a render target
 read a snapshot

An in-between present draws the older frame's draws into the shown
framebuffer again. A draw that samples another render target (a blur, a
glow, a shadow or screen composite) named the target's one sampled copy,
and by then that copy held the target's last picture: a later pass's, or
the next frame's. Games reuse one scratch buffer for several passes a
frame, so every in-between frame composited the wrong picture. Measured
with <prefix>_CHECK_REPLAY on a game that does this (one battle stage):
43 to 86% of the pixels of a frame drawn again differed from the frame as
drawn; a downsampled copy of the scene showed in the top-left corner and
the glow and blur passes changed every other present.

While frames are recorded for drawing again, a draw into a shown
framebuffer that samples a render target now samples a snapshot of the
part of the target it reads (the texture's rectangle and a pixel of
margin), taken when it draws and kept until no recorded frame can name it
(three flips). Snapshots that hold the same pixels are shared, images are
reused, and there are at most 48. The same check now finds 0 pixels that
differ. Draws into other targets, and every draw without interpolation,
sample the target's copy as before.

<prefix>_NO_FB_SNAPSHOTS samples the one copy, as before.
